.TH RUFUSARM64-DEVICE-BACKUP 1 "26 July 2026" "RufusArm64 0.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rufusarm64-device-backup \- save a verified drive image or mounted-filesystem ISO remaster
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rufusarm64-device-backup
.B --device
.I /dev/DEVICE
.B --output
.I /absolute/path/drive.img
.RI [ --format " raw|vhd|vhdx" ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B rufusarm64-device-backup
.B --device
.I /dev/DEVICE
.B --output
.I /absolute/path/filesystem.iso
.B --format iso
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B rufusarm64-device-backup
.B --device
.I /dev/DEVICE
.B --output
.I /absolute/path/drive.vhdx
.B --format vhdx
.B --dry-run
.RI [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rufusarm64-device-backup
supports two deliberately separate capture models.
.PP
The
.BR raw ,
.BR vhd ,
and
.B vhdx
formats capture the exact reported capacity of one explicitly selected whole
block device. The source is opened read-only and exclusively, bound to its
kernel identity and capacity, and revalidated before and after every externally
executed container phase.
.PP
The
.B iso
format remasters exactly one unambiguous mounted filesystem from the selected
whole disk. It does not capture a partition table, hidden sectors, boot records,
unallocated space, additional filesystems, or every physical source byte. A
successful ISO/UDF capture does not establish bootability.
.PP
The default
.B raw
format is a byte-for-byte image. The
.B vhd
and
.B vhdx
formats are dynamic sparse containers created by a trusted, root-owned
.B qemu-img
utility. The source and private output are passed to that utility only through
inherited file descriptors. Backing files, compression, encryption, salvage,
shared-write, fixed containers, and caller-supplied converter options are not
accepted.
.PP
Container capture hashes the held raw source, converts into an owner-only
same-directory temporary file, compares guest-visible bytes against the held
source, performs the supported non-repairing consistency check, hashes and
synchronizes the container, and revalidates the source before publication.
VHDX requires a successful consistency check. QEMU does not provide that check
for VHD, so the report records it as unsupported rather than claiming it ran.
.PP
ISO capture first inventories the live mounted filesystem through descriptor-
relative traversal. Symlinks, hard links, special files, mount crossings,
case-insensitive name collisions, unsupported names, active mutation, and
resource-limit violations are refused. After authentication, RufusArm64 creates
a private read-only bind view with
.BR ro ,
.BR nosuid ,
.BR nodev ,
and
.B noexec
flags and requires its complete supported-content digest to match the plan.
.PP
For non-interactive and graphical ISO capture, the dry-run source binding and
supported-content SHA-256 values are supplied to the privileged helper. The
helper repeats the live inventory and refuses any binding or content difference
before creating the private source view or destination temporary file.
.PP
A trusted, root-owned
.B genisoimage
provider receives only the held source descriptor and a fixed ISO9660 level-3,
Joliet, and UDF policy. Ambient startup configuration, caller-supplied mastering
flags, graft points, excludes, boot images, and final output pathnames are not
accepted. Provider output is bounded and written directly to an owner-only
same-directory temporary file.
.PP
After mastering, the source view is inventoried again. The private image is
mounted independently as UDF with read-only safety flags. Every supported path,
type, regular-file size, and file-content digest is compared. Filesystem-specific
directory allocation sizes are deliberately excluded. The image is then
unmounted and rehashed, and the source is checked once more. Only matching
evidence may proceed to publication.
.PP
Every completed image is published atomically without replacement only after
all required checks succeed. Existing destination files are never replaced.
Incomplete output is removed after failure or cancellation. A failed unmount
preserves its private root-owned workspace rather than recursively deleting a
mounted tree.
.PP
The destination must reside on a different physical disk from the selected
source. Raw capture requires the source capacity. VHD and VHDX dry-run planning
requires the complete source capacity plus a 12.5% policy reserve, with a
minimum 64 MiB margin. ISO planning uses the supported file-content size plus a
conservative mastering reserve and never promises sparse savings.
.PP
Raw, VHD, and VHDX capture normally unmount eligible removable filesystems
before opening the whole device. ISO capture requires exactly one mounted source
filesystem and never uses
.BR --no-unmount .
.SH GRAPHICAL WORKFLOW
Select a removable drive and choose
.BR "Save drive image…" .
Choose Raw image, Dynamic VHD, Dynamic VHDX, or Filesystem ISO/UDF before
selecting a new destination. RufusArm64 runs an unprivileged plan and displays
the exact source, format, destination, conservative required bytes, available
bytes, and format-specific evidence.
.PP
For raw, VHD, and VHDX, authentication requires the phrase
.B SAVE /dev/DEVICE TO /absolute/path/drive.ext .
For ISO capture, the exact mounted child and mountpoint also participate:
.B SAVE FILESYSTEM /dev/NODE AT /mount/path ON /dev/DEVICE TO /absolute/path/filesystem.iso .
.PP
ISO progress distinguishes private source-view creation, inventory, mastering,
source revalidation, independent UDF mounting, content comparison, and atomic
publication. A successful report includes source binding and content digests,
output SHA-256 and size, UDF validation, content-comparison status, and the
published path.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --device " /dev/DEVICE"
Select the whole source disk. Partitions and paths outside
.I /dev
are refused.
.TP
.BI --output " PATH"
Absolute path for the new image file. The path must not already exist. ISO
capture requires a
.I .iso
suffix.
.TP
.BI --format " raw|vhd|vhdx|iso"
Select the output format. The default is
.BR raw .
VHD and VHDX are dynamic sparse containers with fixed converter policy. ISO is
a mounted-filesystem remaster with fixed ISO9660/Joliet/UDF policy.
.TP
.BI --expected-identity " TOKEN"
Require the exact whole-device identity returned by
.BR "rufusarm64-cli list --json" .
This is mandatory with
.B --yes
or
.BR --allow-fixed .
.TP
.BI --expected-source-node " /dev/NODE"
For non-interactive ISO capture, require the exact mounted filesystem device
node returned by the dry-run plan.
.TP
.BI --expected-source-mount " /mount/path"
For non-interactive ISO capture, require the exact mounted filesystem path
returned by the dry-run plan.
.TP
.BI --expected-source-binding-sha256 " DIGEST"
For non-interactive ISO capture, require the exact source-binding SHA-256 from
the dry-run plan. This binds path, type, metadata, inode and mount evidence for
the reviewed live inventory.
.TP
.BI --expected-source-content-sha256 " DIGEST"
For non-interactive ISO capture, require the exact supported-content SHA-256
from the dry-run plan. This authenticates every admitted path, type,
regular-file size, file-content digest, and the reviewed source's directory
metadata across administrator authentication. Cross-filesystem UDF comparison
excludes directory allocation sizes because they are filesystem-specific.
.TP
.BI --volume-id " IDENTIFIER"
Select the ISO volume identifier. Only 1 to 32 uppercase ASCII letters, digits,
and underscores are accepted. The default is
.BR RUFUSARM64 .
.TP
.B --dry-run
Validate source metadata, exact mounted-filesystem inventory when applicable,
trusted provider availability, destination storage, conservative reserve,
disk separation, and collision state without creating a private mount or output
file.
.TP
.B --json
Emit one deterministic JSON plan or report. Whole-device reports use their
schema-2 contract; filesystem ISO plans and reports use their dedicated schema-1
contracts.
.TP
.B --progress-json
Emit bounded schema-2 phase-labelled progress records on standard error while
retaining the final deterministic report on standard output.
.TP
.B --yes
Skip terminal confirmation. Whole-device formats require an expected identity.
ISO additionally requires the expected source node, mountpoint, source-binding
SHA-256, and supported-content SHA-256 from the dry-run plan.
.TP
.B --allow-fixed
Permit a non-removable whole disk for a direct terminal invocation. Root-disk
and protected-mount refusals remain in force. Graphical capture remains limited
to normal removable sources.
.TP
.B --no-unmount
For raw, VHD, and VHDX, refuse mounted filesystems instead of unmounting eligible
removable mounts. This option is rejected for ISO capture.
.TP
.BR --version , " -v"
Print the installed version.
.SH SAFETY
The source is never opened for writing. Whole-device formats use a held block-
device descriptor. ISO uses a dedicated read-only bind view of the exact
reviewed mountpoint and repeats descriptor-rooted inventories around mastering
and validation. External providers receive descriptor paths rather than the
selected source or final destination pathname. No final pathname appears before
verified completion. Manually created image files remain user data after package
removal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rufusarm64 (1),
.BR rufusarm64-cli (1),
.BR rufusarm64-device-qualify (1)
